From 5ba22c1d9ab0db67b7353373fc5daddd98e7f47e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: srymsjackson <jstrazzo22@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:13:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Dependency audit: patch 5 packages with known CVEs, add
 version-floor tests

pip-audit found 20 known CVEs across aiohttp, starlette, python-dotenv, and
python-multipart at the versions previously pinned. Bumped:

  aiohttp          3.14.1 -> 3.14.3
  python-dotenv    1.0.1  -> 1.2.2
  python-multipart 0.0.9  -> 0.0.32
  fastapi          0.115.0 -> 0.118.0  (see below)
  starlette        0.38.6  -> 0.48.0   (see below)

fastapi and starlette had to move together: fastapi==0.115.0 pins
starlette<0.39.0, and 0.38.6 -- the version we were on -- is already the
newest patch release in that range, so none of starlette's CVE fixes were
reachable without bumping fastapi too. Went with the smallest fastapi bump
that unlocks a patched starlette range (0.118.0 -> starlette<0.49.0) rather
than jumping to current-latest fastapi (0.141.x at time of writing), to keep
this a low-risk dependency patch rather than a framework migration.

This closes the one finding with real reachable exposure in this app:
PYSEC-2026-1943, unbounded in-memory buffering of multipart text fields.
app/security.py's verify_twilio_signature() calls request.form() to build
params for the HMAC check before the signature itself is validated -- so
this parsing happens for any POST to /voice/incoming, /voice/collect, or
/voice/status regardless of whether the caller has a valid signature,
making it reachable by unauthenticated traffic on public webhook endpoints,
not just authenticated routes.

Starlette still has unpatched findings requiring starlette>=1.0.1 (Host
header URL-reconstruction issues, a Windows-only StaticFiles SSRF, an
HTTPEndpoint dispatch quirk, a FileResponse Range-header DoS), which needs
a much larger fastapi jump. Checked each against this app's actual code:
no StaticFiles, no FileResponse, no HTTPEndpoint routing, and the two
places that do read request.url (app/main.py's login-redirect builder,
app/security.py's Twilio signature URL) don't use it for a security
decision -- security.py explicitly builds the signature-check URL from the
trusted configured BASE_URL, not request.url, precisely to avoid trusting
attacker-influenced request data. Judged the residual risk low enough not
to justify a framework-version jump in the same patch as everything else
here; worth a dedicated pass later with its own full regression testing,
particularly given app.on_event is already deprecated and may require
migrating to lifespan handlers at some point.

Also converted requirements.txt from UTF-16 to UTF-8 -- the original
encoding was invisible in a normal editor but broke grep/git diff/every
text tool that assumes UTF-8, which is how the dependency versions had to
be read via a manual UTF-16 decode during this audit in the first place.
No content changed beyond that and the 5 version bumps.

Tests: tests/test_dependency_versions.py (5 new tests) -- asserts each
patched package is at or above its fixed-version floor, so a future
accidental downgrade (e.g. resolving a dependency conflict by pinning an
older version) fails CI instead of silently reintroducing a fixed CVE.
Verified by downgrading starlette back to 0.38.6 and re-running: fails as
expected, with a message pointing at why. Full suite: 105/105 passing,
verified via a completely clean venv installed only from the final
requirements.txt (not just the dev venv these changes were made in).
